        <body>
            <div xml:id="d126">

                <pb facs="0296" n="196" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg196"/>

                <head>No. 126.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    Sargent.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-04-16"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 16,
                        1884</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 227.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your No. 263, of the 24th ultimo,
                    transmitting the text of a recent ministerial circular touching the danger of
                    consuming raw pork as food, has been received.</p>
                <p>I am disposed to regard this circular as evincing a willingness on the part of
                    the German authorities to consider the pork question in a practical way, which
                    cannot but promise good results.</p>
                <p>There can be but little doubt of the fact, to which I called attention in my
                    letter to the President of February 28, transmitting the report of the pork
                    commission, that trichiniasis is distinctly traceable to the consumption of the
                    flesh of <hi rendition="#italic">freshly killed</hi> native hogs, and that the
                    virulence <pb facs="0297" n="197" xml:id="pg197"/> of infection is observed to
                    diminish with the time elapsing between the killing of the animal and the
                    consumption of its flesh, soon ceasing altogether, especially when the meat is
                    even slightly salted.</p>
                <p>The disposition, I am satisfied, exists on both sides to favor the widest,
                    frankest, and most practical examination of the whole subject, in the interest
                    of the people of the two countries.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
